---
// The explanation below the Lab: the data, the symbols, how an SVM finds its
// border, C, curved borders and γ, and the model in short. Static HTML;
// formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="svm-data">
    <h2 id="svm-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      Each shape is a student. Across the graph is how many hours they studied
      before a test; up the graph is how many hours they slept. Circles passed
      and triangles failed. Studying and sleep both help, so the border between
      them runs on a slant, and a few students break the rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      The solid line is the border the model draws. The dashed lines on either
      side are the edges of its margin, like the kerbs of a street. Students
      with a ring are the support vectors. The numbers are made up, and{' '}
      <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows another class.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="svm-symbols">
    <h2 id="svm-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>x</dt>
        <dd>A student: hours studied and hours slept.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>y</dt>
        <dd>+1 for passed, −1 for failed.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">f(x)</dt>
        <dd>
          The model's score. Above 0: pass. Below 0: fail. Exactly ±1 on the
          dashed lines.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>w, b</dt>
        <dd>
          For a straight border, the weights of the two features and the
          intercept.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-error">ξ</dt>
        <dd>
          Slack: how far a student is past their own dashed line. 0 for students
          safely on their side.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-a">C</dt>
        <dd>How much each bit of slack costs.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-b">γ</dt>
        <dd>For a curved border: how far each student's pull reaches.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="svm-how">
    <h2 id="svm-how">How the model finds its border</h2>
    <p>
      Many straight lines could separate the two groups. A support vector
      machine picks the one with the widest street: the line that stays as far
      as possible from the nearest students on both sides.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.linear} />
    <p>
      The street's edges are where f(x) = +1 and −1, so its width is 2 ÷ ‖w‖. A
      wide street means a small w.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.margin} />
    <p>
      Only the students on or inside the street decide where it goes: these are
      the <strong>support vectors</strong>. Drag a student far from the border
      and nothing moves. Drag a support vector and the whole street shifts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="svm-c">
    <h2 id="svm-c">C: a wide street, or few mistakes?</h2>
    <p>
      When the groups overlap, no street is empty. The model lets some students
      stand inside it, or even on the wrong side, and charges C for each unit of
      slack. It then makes the total as small as it can:
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.objective} />
    <p>
      A <strong>small C</strong> makes slack cheap: the street gets wide and
      many students lean on it, so the border depends on many of them and stays
      calm. A<strong>large C</strong> makes slack expensive: the street narrows
      to squeeze students out of it, and a single odd student can swing the
      border.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="svm-curved">
    <h2 id="svm-curved">Curved borders and γ</h2>
    <p>
      Turn on <strong>Curved</strong> and the border may bend. Instead of a
      straight line, each support vector pulls the score towards its own side,
      strongly when it is close and weakly when it is far:
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.rbf} />
    <p>
      γ sets how far that pull reaches. A small γ gives each student a wide
      reach, so the border stays smooth and close to straight. A large γ gives
      each student a tiny reach, so the border wraps around single students and
      makes small islands. That fits these students but would guess badly for
      new ones.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="svm-summary">
    <h2 id="svm-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Draw</strong> the border with the widest street between the
        groups.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Allow slack</strong> for students inside the street, at a price
        C.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keep</strong> only the support vectors: they alone hold the
        street in place.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bend</strong> the border with a curved kernel when a straight
        one cannot fit, and use γ to keep it from bending too much.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
